; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What everything on two legs moves by — every monster and every hero.
;
; Declared once, here, and linked from each of them: Animations = Humanoid in a
; Monster or Hero block, the way a unit links the Effect it wears.
;
; The creature kit ships rigged models with no animation; the animation library
; ships animation with no creature. They meet because both were built on the same
; standard humanoid skeleton — every joint the monsters have, the library
; animates. So one set moves the whole bestiary, and a new monster needs no
; animation work at all.
;
; The clips named here are the defaults. A unit's block may name its own Idle,
; Walk, Attack, Hurt or Death instead — the runner jogs rather than walks, and
; each hero stands and strikes his own way. Only the ones that need to differ
; do: every monster strikes the same way on purpose, so a blow is recognisable
; whichever of them threw it.
;
; Idle and Walk are STATES and loop. Attack and Hurt are MOMENTS and play once,
; over whatever the creature was doing: the blow when its weapon lets go, the
; flinch when its health drops. Nothing in a snapshot says "is swinging right
; now", and a moment played on a loop is not the same thing slower.
;
; A clip is fetched by name, so a name that is not in the library simply leaves
; that monster standing still rather than failing. The library's own names are
; listed in the pack, and DungeonMonsterArtTest checks every name against them.
;
; A unit that links no set stands in its modelling pose, which is what the
; monsters did before this existed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AnimationSet
  Name = Humanoid
  Libraries = [
    ; Everything on two legs down here is built on one rig. That is not a saving
    ; so much as the reason the bestiary is worth swapping: one set of clips moves
    ; the archer, the minion and the thing at the bottom of the last floor.
    ;
    ; Several files, because a kit sorts its clips by what the movement is for. No
    ; clip a unit plays is in two of them, so one that does not need a file loses
    ; nothing by having it.
    animations/characters/general.glb,
    animations/characters/movement.glb,
    animations/characters/melee.glb,
    ; And the ranged ones, now that two of them shoot rather than swing.
    animations/characters/ranged.glb,
    ; And the spells, for whoever casts them.
    animations/characters/magic.glb,
  ]
  Idle = Idle_A
  Walk = Walking_A
  ; An overhead chop: the arm goes up and comes down. Everything down here
  ; strikes the same way, from the smallest to the boss, so a blow reads as a
  ; blow wherever it comes from — a monster that merely twitches at you does not
  ; look like it hit anything, whatever the health bar says.
  ;
  ; Played ONCE, on the frame the weapon lets go — not on a loop while the
  ; monster "is attacking", which is true from the moment it notices the hero
  ; until the fight ends. The clip used to run on its own tempo, so a monster
  ; that strikes every 1.6 seconds threw a one-second punch and none of them was
  ; the blow.
  ;
  ; One-handed, because that is what every one of them carries: a blade, an axe
  ; or a staff, hung on the bone the rig keeps for it.
  Attack = Melee_1H_Attack_Chop
  ; Played once as it falls, before the body is taken away.
  Death = Death_A
  ; Hurt = Hit_A
  ;
  ; A flinch when something takes health off it. OFF on purpose, not forgotten:
  ; a flinch and a swing are the same one-shot channel, so a monster being shot
  ; while it swings drops the swing — the hit it was landing simply stops being
  ; drawn, and hitting a monster reads as cancelling its attack. It comes back
  ; as something the player buys rather than as something every arrow does for
  ; free. Naming it here is all it takes to switch on.
End
